  "slug": "bacterial-genetic-32fc",
  "summary": "Recent sequencing methods pair new analysis tools with reference testing, including GIAB phasing benchmarks and CITE-seq and bulk TCR-seq comparisons.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is software and workflow infrastructure for sequencing data, with most items evaluated against reference datasets or established tools. Long-read and nanopore work recurs: a Snakemake workflow benchmarks phasing methods on GIAB to build haplotype-resolved proteomes, Cornetto uses selective nanopore sequencing to target unsolved regions for near-complete diploid genomes, and the direct RNA study benchmarks RNA004 chemistry with new pseudouridine and m6A base-calling models. Single-cell and preprocessing tools show the same pattern: Amaranth performs UMI-aware transcript assembly, TIRTL-seq is benchmarked against bulk and 10x single-cell TCR-seq, and CountASAP is benchmarked against CITE-seq tools. What appears new is the explicit pairing of method development with reference testing. ConSeqUMI resolves individual molecules via UMI consensus, and structure-aware models predict nanopore ionic currents for noncanonical XNA bases. The thread is dominant rather than universal.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
